[Music]  william reed was the jackie robinson of  hockey the first black black player in  the nhl here he is at 22 years old in  the 1950s the late 1950s as a rookie  forward for the boston bruins since then  he's continued to be a powerful  influence on the sport promoting hockey  and minority communities now his  contributions are being recognized in  boston and on capitol hill major garrett  spoke with ore and shares his remarkable  story  boston went oh re down the ice willie  o'reilly became the first black player  in the national hockey league in 1958  suiting up for the boston bruins and  it's 3-1 o'ree now 86 was playing minor  league hockey in quebec when he got the  call on january the 18th 1958  the bruins called the quebec case and  says we want to re  to meet the bruins in montreal to play  two games against montreal canadians oh  reid did what jackie robinson had done  11 years earlier integrate a white sport  as a black professional they sat me down  and said willie we brought you up  because we think you can add a little  something to the club don't worry about  anything else about just going out and  playing your game the speedy winger did  and afterward described it this way it  was the greatest sound of my life i  believe i'll always remember this day  i'm going to ask you a question i i  think in the first period you look like  you're a little nervous were you yes i  bet it scared you a little yes it did oh  ree was talking about achieving his  ultimate goal playing in the nhl not  making racial history i didn't recognize  that i i had broke the colorbar until i  read it in the paper the next day  did you encounter any racial hostility  oh yeah you know from uh from different  players and fans in the stand different  players on yeah on the opposition yeah i  heard the racial remarks and um racial  slurs and it was in chicago and new york  uh probably the the worst problems i had  black players in pro sports knew what to  expect and were expected to endure it  in silence in terms of this business of  being the jackie robinson of hockey have  you had any troubles  no none none that you could uh say that  were troubles i've heard a few jeers  like that but i guess all hockey players  yeah antoree goes back for boston o'ree  played 45 games in the nhl over two  seasons then flourished in minor league  hockey winning two scoring titles i  remember the first time i came into this  building in san diego where o'ree played  eight seasons his number is the only one  in the rafters next week are we getting  set the boston bruins will retire oree's  number 22. i'm over overwhelmed and  really really excited but kelly i'm  i'm really thrilled o'ree is already in  the hockey hall of fame he has a statue  at the national museum of african  american history and culture and he is  on the verge of receiving the  congressional gold medal he really is  somebody that exemplifies courage and  you know what we want as a role model  for our kids senator debbie stabenow of  michigan and congressman mike quigley of  illinois have led the gold medal effort  congress's highest civilian award when  you started talking to your colleagues  did any of them know who willie was no  how about you mike very very few knew  who he was i'll be honest  i grew up not knowing who willie was was  it a hard sell once you had the  conversation no in fact people would go  the first black player  and the nhl wow and when he went to the  chicago stadium as a boston bruin the  fans were the worst in the league and so  were the players it's tough to hear  because these were my heroes growing up  this story it has a twist i had an  unfortunate accident willie o'reilly the  nhl's first black player a descendant of  slaves played his entire professional  career  blind in his right eye it was a slap  shot from the point the [ __ ] him up and  hit me flat in the right eye and broke  broke my nose o-rie surgeon said he'd  never play hockey again but within weeks  he was back on the ice striving for the  nhl he swore his family to secrecy and  never told any team of his blindness i  said forget about what you can't see and  just concentrate on what you can see  what he couldn't see then was the  enduring power of his example you can  only imagine what he's been through  anthony declare plays forward for the  florida panthers i was a young black  male playing hockey in a predominantly  white sport  you know you don't see too many black  athletes therefore you motivated me i  would say to make the nhl and try to you  know pass it on forward to the next  generation for sure uri has spent the  last 25 years passing it forward to the  next generation as one of the nhl's  diversity ambassadors bringing hockey to  underserved minority communities this is  what i tell these kids you can do  anything you set your mind to do and  don't let anybody tell you you can't  attain your goal if you feel strongly  within your heart and within your mind  and never give up  willie did some of that outreach right  here at fort dupont ice rink in  washington dc william reid is quite  obviously a national hero he's also a  personal hero of mine i grew up watching  him play for the san diego goals as a  child in san diego william inspired me  to pick up the sport and we have some  photographic evidence of that there you  see it yours truly age 14 fully suited  up san diego skyline in the background  to meet william with one of the great  privileges of my life and an honor as a  hockey player a san diegan and an  american gayle  wow yours truly still looking pretty  good there major you look fantastic and  look good on that nice thing yeah  don't go anywhere but i want to go back  to willie o'ree and then i want to go  back to major's picture number one brian  mullen who knows the thing about hockey  in our studio has been talking about  willie o'reilly for years  but it's something about when you see  all of the things that he went through  did in that interview he seemed to  downplay the racism that he experienced  and now now he's talking about it major  which i also think is very happy very  healthy and very important to do imagine  what this man went through and was blind  on in one eye that's extraordinary  exactly the blindness is one aspect of  the story but all the racial slurs and  hostility he encountered he knew he  simply had to endure it that was the  only option it was the only option for  jackie robinson it was the only option  for willie o'ree and he really didn't  ever talk about it then he's not so  comfortable talking about it now he will  recognize it acknowledge it but say i  push through it and i tell children from  minority communities don't worry about  it pursue your dreams  work hard believe in your heart that you  can achieve things and all things are  possible  and major can i say you look really good  on skates but i'm so glad you got the  skills yes you still got skills you got  skills  and you were so cute i played as a child  very cute  it's still very plain  so  so that rink in san diego willie's pla  played there i played there he skated on  this rank i've skated on this ring so  it's all together very cool

